e,Z/his book is not going to emphasize transits, because there are already many good books on the subject. An excellent and very thorough book on transits is Rob Hand's Planets in Transit. Here are some key things regarding transiting planets that I have observed in my years of practicing astrology
When I start a lecture on transits I always state, "When you do a chart, you stop the planets in the heavens for the exact moment, and latitude and longitude, of birth. This is the natal chart. But these planets keep moving through the universe. As they continue to move they are called transiting planets." The natal chart describes our destiny, potential, tendencies, etc. It's our road map, but it takes a transit to trigger events that our chart has promised. Transits are the day-to-day positions of the planets in the heavens as shown in the ephemeris and are important in

how they affect the natal chart.
152 / Transits
There are two important things to remember when dealing with transits. First, a transit cannot bring what is not promised. Look at the natal aspects to determine what to expect. Secondly, the slower the transit, the more the effect will be felt and the more it will change your life. Therefore, a Pluto transit has the greatest effect and the transiting Moon has the least impact. The conjunction and opposition are the most powerful transits, followed by the squares and trines. Every conjunction will affect you in a personal way. The opposition involves relationships between you and someone else. Squares can test you and present obstacles and roadblocks. Trines and sextiles are what we wait for, bringing lovely times, opportunities, and benefits.
When you evaluate a transit, any transit, you have to examine the
nature of the transiting planet. Jupiter expands. Saturn is the reality factor. Uranus is all about freedom. Neptune dissolves, and Pluto breaks down and rebuilds. Not only is it important to keep these things in mind, it is also important to remember that a transiting planet will carry with it the influence of the house and sign that it's in and the house that it rules in the natal chart. I will give you an example. I have Saturn ruling my first house, so every Saturn transit affects me personally. I have Capricorn rising with Saturn in the first house, so that is doubly true. I have Jupiter in my eighth house, so every Jupiter transit affects me in an eighth-house manner. The transiting planet will always carry with it the nature of where it is placed in your chart—the nature of the sign and the house that it rules. You have to look at these factors with every single transit. I look at transits psychologically, spiritually, and in an event-oriented way. I ask myself, "How will this transit change the person? What lessons will be learned? How is this transit providing opportunities for the client to evolve? And what sort of events will happen?" It is important to look at the transits in all these ways and then synthesize it.
Rules for Transits
Every transit will carry the flavor of the natal house placement of the transiting planet. For example, if natal Saturn is placed in your second house, every Saturn transit will affect your finances, your values, and your sense of self-worth.
Every transit will carry the flavor of the house the transiting planet rules. For instance, if Saturn rules your natal eighth house, then every Saturn transit will deal with psychological reflection, joint finances, taxes,

sex, etc.
Every transit will carry the flavor of the transiting planet's natural rulership. For example, since Saturn is the natural ruler of the tenth house, every Saturn transit will somehow impact career, public image, reputation, a parent, or other tenth-house matters.
Every transit will carry the flavor of the sign the transiting planet is in. There is a great difference between a Saturn-in-Taurus transit and a Saturn-in-Aries transit. For a fixed sign (like Taurus), the results will be slower to manifest, possibly delayed or denied, and longer lasting in duration. This will be the situation for a Saturn-in-Taurus transit.
If your natal planet that is being transited is well aspected, your gains are greater and your losses mitigated. If it is poorly aspected, your gains are fewer and your losses are greater.
If your natal planet is well placed by sign or house, the outcome of the transit can be very positive. For example, if you have natal Saturn in the tenth house or in Capricorn, there will be greater benefit than if your natal Saturn was poorly placed or in detriment or fall. For example, natal Saturn in the fourth house or in Cancer is not favorable, because Saturn is weak there.
154 / Transits
A transit setting off a progression is of major importance. It can activate a progression early or reactivate a progression that may seem to have passed.
A transit setting off an eclipse is also of major importance. A transiting planet hitting an eclipse point, either before or after the eclipse occurs, will activate the eclipse.
Neptune and Pluto transits manifest in a fashion similar to a progression, due to the long length of the transit. The same rules we have for progressions would almost apply to these transits, because they last for such a long time. Transits of Pluto, Neptune, and Uranus have a fated quality to them. They work on the subconscious level as well in an event-oriented way.
Minor transits can be very important under certain circumstances. Rules for Minor Transits
Minor transits can be very important when they trigger a progression. They can be quite significant if the transiting planet is in the progression. For example, if the progressed Sun is conjunct natal Venus, either the transiting Sun or transiting Venus can be significant.
Minor transits can be very important when they trigger a major

transit. Major transits involve Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune, or Pluto.
Minor transits can be very important when they trigger an eclipse. Any transiting planet can trigger an eclipse point. Mars, in particular, transiting over that degree can give you a hint of what the eclipse will bring in your chart.
Minor transits portend minor events. Good minor transits can signal a time when you may receive a small gift, get an important phone call, give a great party, receive a nice com-
pliment, or just generally have a good day. Difficult minor transits can indicate a bad haircut, a spat with your spouse, a small disruption at work, or just generally having a bad day.
A minor transit can be significant if it triggers a shared-experience tie between two people involved in a relationship. A shared-experience tie occurs when each person has a planet at the same degree. For instance, one person may have natal Venus exactly on the other person s natal Sun. This is a very nice love tie, and if it is triggered by a minor transit, the two people concerned will be very loving toward each other that day.
Rules for Orbs of Transiting Planets
Orbs are very important and vary greatly between the faster and slower-moving transiting planets.
The orb of the transiting Moon is just a few hours long. Only the few hours in which it is applying in aspect have merit.
Sun, Mercury, and Venus transits tend to work the day before the transit approaches the planet and the day it is exact, but rarely as it leaves the planet.
Mars is referred to as the timekeeper, but many times I find it works a degree early.
Jupiter transits start working several degrees early. You feel the optimism and buoyancy of Jupiter 5° or 6° approaching, but this diminishes when Jupiter is 2° past the planet.
The Saturn influence is felt 3° or 4° as it approaches. It is very intense 2° before, but often when it is exact you seem to have a reprieve (during hard Saturn transits). Then a degree or two later, you experience a whiplash effect.
