Saturn Transits
Saturn is known as the teacher, the taskmaster. Under difficult transits, it can inhibit, restrict, delay, cause fear and mistrust, and be separative. Positive aspects can improve your concentration, steady your emotions, and can be a self-disciplining factor and create a firm foundation wherever it is transiting. When I talk to my clients or students about Saturn, I describe it as the "heavy hand" of reality. Saturn defines and creates realism wherever it transits. Marc Robertson wrote a great book on the cycles of Saturn called Transit of Saturn. I find it is a must for every astrologer's library.
When Saturn transits the first house, enters the sign Aries, or aspects Mars, you can be defined as an individual—a serious and dedicated one. It may define new goals and bring about a clearer approach to life and self-discipline. Healthier eating and exercising become practices that last. Often, habits that are incorporated in your life when Saturn goes through the first house become habits for a lifetime. When Saturn crosses from the twelfth house to the first house, something is usually left behind from the past, and life is approached in a different way We can let go of a marriage, a job, or a responsibility. Self-expression may be more diff'icult when Saturn is in the first house, and we may appear to be more serious and thoughtful. Saturn in Aries will tame the impetuous-ness of Aries.
When Saturn was transiting through Aries, many Aries remarked at that
time, "I feel as if someone is raining on my parade." Whenever Saturn and Mars come together there is a stop-go kind of energy. Mars wants to forge ahead and Saturn says you must be cautious. What usually happens is a tremendous amount is accomplished in a very short time because of a great spurt of energy, followed by a standstill and near collapse after the project is complete. A Saturn/Mars aspect natally can suggest some frustration in life, and when Saturn transits in
