Stationing and Retrograde Planets
C/C][ of the planets except for the Sun and Moon (which are the luminaries) will go retrograde for certain periods of time. Retrograde motion is the apparent backward motion of a planet. When a planet goes retrograde, it is not actually going backward in its orbit, but it appears to be moving backward from our perspective on Earth. The planet appears to slow down to change direction, and it actually appears to stop or pause in the heavens. When it pauses or stops, it is said to be stationing or to be stationary. At that time there is a magnification of the expression of that planet, and its energy is greatly intensified. It is very powerful and can be very potent if it keys, or ties, into your chart.

184 / Stationing and Retrograde Planets Rules for Stationing and Retrograde Planets
The intensity of energy begins when a planet starts to slow down to change direction, either to go retrograde or direct. It is felt 3° or 4° before and after the degree at which it stations. The main heightening of energy is felt at the degree of stationing. For example, Neptune just moved a degree past the point where it stopped to change direction and go retrograde, and I felt confusion, disorientation, idealism, etc. I expect when it moves 2° or 3° past the stationing degree that I will feel more clarity and realism.
You start to feel the planet's intense energy as it slows down to change direction. You feel it during the 3° or 4° as it approaches the point of

stationing, and you feel it very intensely the entire time it is at that degree when it retrogrades. When it moves a degree away, the intensity lessens. At 2° or 3° away from the stationing point, the experience and feelings are greatly diminished.
Like all transits, a retrograding planet will carry with it the influence of the sign and house it is transiting, the house it rules in your natal chart, and the house it rules naturally. Matters pertaining to these areas will be greatly intensified, many times to crisis proportion. Just remember that it is not necessarily good or bad energy. It is just different.
Again, go back to the natal promise. It makes a difference if the planet is retrograde or direct in your chart. For example. I worked on committees with a friend who had Mercury retrograde natally. When Mercury would go retrograde by transit, she was the only one who felt comfortable with the energy and was very effective in getting things done, whereas those of us with Mercury direct in our natal chart had to do everything three times.
If you have Mercury direct at birth and it goes retrograde by progression, the transiting retrograde energy will react differently in your own chart. I have noticed this because Mercury is stationing and has turned retrograde by progression in my chart. The last time Mercury went retrograde by transit, I wrote comfortably
and things flowed more easily. I was focused and I could concentrate more effectively.
When my progressed Mercury got within a degree of going retrograde, I no longer wanted to counsel, lecture, and teach astrology or go on radio or television (the outward expression of direct Mercury) as often. But I started to write, do research, and do more introspective things (the inward expression of a retrograde Mercury). I find myself doing more solitary things like gardening and creating beauty, such as making miniature dollhouses for my grandchildren and, of course, writing.
A classic example of this is a client of mine who was a professor at a university. When Mercury by progression went retrograde, he gave up his job as a professor and became a writer for a national newspaper.
A stationing planet will be incredibly important when it sets off a heavy transit, an eclipse point, or a progression. It brings the energy to a head. A number of things start to happen to bring the issue to culmination for good or bad. If it is a good progression and the planet stationing is making a harmonious aspect, you could receive a windfall. But if it is a difficult progression and a planet stations and triggers it, you

could have a crisis or difficulty— a "crisis" meaning an area of life that needs a lot of attention, not necessarily bad or good. It is just a time to take care of business.
