Birth Data —The date of birth, the moment (the exact time) of birth, and the longitude and latitude of birth are considered the birth data. This information is needed to construct a birth chart.
Cycle —A cycle is the planetary movement around the entire circle of

signs. Pluto is the slowest-moving planet and takes 248 years to cycle the entire zodiac. The Sun's cycle takes one year.
Degree —The horoscope has 360°, and each sign has 30°. Degrees are used to measure exact positions of planets and other astrological factors. Eclipse —An eclipse is a phenomena that involves the Sun, Moon, and
Earth. Eclipses stimulate change in the area of the horoscope in which they fall. There are two distinct types of eclipses: Solar Eclipses and Lunar Eclipses.
Electional Astrology —The branch of astrology that specializes in choosing the most auspicious time to plan an event or begin a new project. Electional astrology is used by astrologers to help clients set marriage dates, start new businesses, plan important events, and so on.
Ephemeris —A book of tables showing the exact positions of the Sun, Moon, and planets on a daily basis.
Full Moon —The opposition between the Sun and the Moon. The time when the Moon is 180° from the Sun.
Hard Aspect —Planets that are 120° apart are called square aspects. Planets that are 180° apart are called opposition aspects. These aspects are considered difficult and create tremendous tension. Planets in close degree in the same house or sign are called conjunctions. Some of these conjunctions can be considered hard aspects, depending on the planets involved.
Horoscope —Another term for birth chart. The word horoscope comes from a Greek word meaning "map of the hour."
House Cusp —The first degree of any house in a horoscope or birth chart.
Lunar Eclipse —The Lunar Eclipse occurs at the Full Moon. This happens about two times per year, when the Earth is exactly between the Moon and Sun, and the Earth's shadow on the Moon obscures the normal Full Moon.
Lunation —The New Moon and the Full Moon, which occur every month, are referred to as lunations.
Major Aspect —The major aspects in astrology are the conjunction, sextile, trine, square, and opposition.
Midheaven —The sign on the cusp of the tenth house of the horoscope is referred to as the Midheaven. Another term for Midheaven is Medium Coeli.
Minor Aspect —Some minor aspects in astrology are the semi-square (two planets 45° apart), the semisextile (two planets 30° apart), and the

quincunx (two planets 150° apart).
New Moon —The conjunction of the Sun and the Moon. The time when the Moon is at the exact same degree as the Sun.
Nodes of the Moon —The nodes of the Moon are intersections of the Moon's orbit around the Earth with the ecliptic. They are considered to be of karmic significance.
North Node —The house and sign in which the North Node is placed define the area of life that you are striving to develop. This node has somewhat of a Jupiter effect.
Orb —Refers to the range of degrees within which an aspect is considered to be operating. The closer the two planets are in degree, or orb, the stronger or more potent the aspect will be. The farther the two planets are in degree, or orb, the weaker the effect will be.
Predictive Astrology —The area of astrology that seeks to forecast future events, cycles, and trends.
266 / Glossary
Ruler —Certain planets have been given rulership over certain signs. In modern astrology, the Sun rules the sign Leo, the Moon rules Cancer, Mercury rules Gemini and Virgo, Venus rules Taurus and Libra, Mars rules Aries (and also has a tie to Scorpio), Jupiter rules Sagittarius, Saturn rules Capricorn, Uranus rules Aquarius, Neptune rules Pisces, and Pluto rules Scorpio.
Separating Aspect —A separating aspect refers to a planet that is moving away from an exact aspect with a slower-moving planet.
Soft Aspect —Planets that are 60° apart are called sextiles. Planets that are 90° apart are called trines. These aspects are comfortable and harmonious, bringing ease and opportunity. Some conjunctions are considered soft aspects, depending on the planets involved.
Solar Eclipse —The Solar Eclipse occurs at the New Moon. This happens about two times per year, when the Moon, in its orbit around the Earth, gets exactly between the Earth and the Sun and obscures the light of the Sun from the vantage point of the Earth.
South Node —The house and sign in which the South Node is placed describe the area of life that you have already mastered in other lifetimes. It is the line of least resistance, and an area of life that may limit or restrict you, as it holds you in old habit patterns. This node has somewhat of a Saturn effect.
Stationary —Refers to a time when a transiting planet seems to stand still just before shifting to the opposite direction from which it had been

moving.
Transit —Refers to the current movement of the planets through the signs and houses of the horoscope.
Transpersonal Planets —The transpersonal planets, or higher-octave planets, are planets in our solar system that are not visible from the Earth without a telescope. They include Uranus, Neptune, and Pluto.
VoiD-OF-couRSE MooN—When the Moon has reached a late degree in a sign, and will not make any more aspects to planets before it leaves that sign, it is considered to be void-of-course. In an event chart, this suggests a situation that will be held in abeyance.
